Track 2: Salty Tongue, Sweet Gums
- Story & Context: Miri sits on the edge of the submerged pier, her fingers calloused from years of plucking strings, confessing to a stranger that she prefers the taste of seawater to the filtered tap of the high-rises. She explains how the constant, rhythmic thrum of the city’s pumps beneath her bed became her only lullaby, leading her to write songs that mimic the mechanical heartbeat of the district. "I chew on the rust just to taste the machine," she whispers, her voice cracking as the neuro-bass swells behind her to mimic a rushing current.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up of a young woman with sea-salt in her hair, looking out over a vast, flooded cityscape at golden hour, wearing a torn safety-orange hood, soft focus, ethereal lighting.

Track 3: The Drift-King’s Wake
- Story & Context: Decades after the first rave, the children of the Harbor Terminal still tell stories of the legendary Drift-King who supposedly turned into a liquid wave during the peak of the bass drop. They say if you play the right frequency on a wooden guitar, the water in the district will briefly stop its encroaching rise, caught in a permanent state of euphoric suspension. "We are the notes that the ocean forgot to swallow," they chant in unison, their voices layered with the distorted, crunching bass that has become the anthem of the reclaimed city.
